    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "↳ one core-made clearing handle",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#ownership",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "1\nMove the clearing mechanism into core.\nSTATED\n\nThe language-neutral scope is reusable. Each TypeScript service still constructs and owns its own scope; algorithms, source cache and project graph stay in their existing roles.\n\nPackage & instance boundaries ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#ownership",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "2\nKeep six independent address spaces.\nSTATED\n\nOne scope, six typed handles, one map per handle. Existing keys and value types stay separate; a shared key never makes two query kinds share an entry.\n\nOpen all six drawers ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#drawers",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "3\nKeep the exact entries and failure behavior.\nSTATED\n\nReuse values and promises, including empties; the new generic primitive also accepts undefined. Rejected promises stay cached; synchronous factory throws are retried. Reference and position locations are still rehydrated on access.\n\nTickets, failures & projections ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#entries",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "4\nFinish refresh before starting a turn.\nSTATED\n\nSuccessful refresh clears all six, even for unchanged files. A failed refresh never reaches that clearing step, preserving the current semantic-cache turn. This ordering already existed.\n\nReplay success or failure ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#lifecycle",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "5\nClear first; await project release next.\nSTATED\n\nClearing stays synchronous. The service now returns a promise and the backend awaits it, so a pending or rejected graph release reaches the caller. The graph’s existing release order is unchanged.\n\nReplay the changed barrier ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#lifecycle",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "6\nClearing is reusable, not terminal.\nUNEXPLAINED\n\nNo turn token, cancellation, closed flag or query lock. Both signals empty the same registered handles; repeated clearing is allowed. Old promises can settle without replacing new entries, and queries can refill during release.\n\nThe analogy’s limits ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#reusable",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "7\nExpose a small cache API; pass fewer inputs.\nUNEXPLAINED\n\nCore exports the scope and lookup-only handle type. Handles expose no clearing operation. The service’s beginTurn takes a file list instead of a full snapshot; this is documented without a separate rationale.\n\nExact public signatures ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#api",
      "type": null
    },
    {
      "tag": "A",
      "text": "8\nCharacterize, then extract.\nSTATED\n\nFour new core tests and six new service tests pin the preservation contract. Five previous service tests remain with their assertions intact. The new pending/rejected-release case uses a graph test double; the real fixture also remains.\n\nTest scope & source receipts ↘",
      "id": "",
      "href": "#evidence",
      "type": null
    },
